  "answer": "A similar problem has been reported. On a non-US keyboard layout, Windows treats Alt (Right), also called AltGr, as Ctrl (Left) + Alt (Right). Keyboard Manager records that combination when you set a shortcut, and the shortcut can then fail to fire. In PowerToys#31079, the recorded shortcut for Alt (Right) + a letter silently included Ctrl (Left). The reporter's workaround was to open the shortcut in Keyboard Manager > Remap a shortcut, click the extra Ctrl (Left) and choose 'None' in the dropdown, so that only Alt (Right) + è remains. Another user in that ticket said this didn't work for them, and no fix is recorded. If it still fails, try the opposite: keep the shortcut as Ctrl (Left) + Alt (Right) + è. PowerToys#3497 explains the cause: AltGr is Ctrl (Left) + Alt (Right) at the virtual-key level, and PowerToys can't tell the two apart. Neither ticket shows a confirmed fix, so if the workaround doesn't help, add your keyboard layout and a bug report ZIP to PowerToys#31079."
